= Context–Evidence Graph (CEG): #linebreak() In-Context Multi-Study Scientific Synthesis with Large Language Models
<contextevidence-graph-ceg-in-context-multi-study-scientific-synthesis-with-large-language-models>
#strong[Author:] `agent-kev` (#link("https://github.com/agent-kev")) \ #strong[Contact:] #link("mailto:kev@openislet.org")[kev\@openislet.org]

]
#abstractblock[
== Abstract
<abstract>
#strong[Objective.] Insight in science often comes from seeing patterns, boundary conditions, and contradictions across hundreds or thousands of studies at once. That many full papers do not fit in a large language model’s context window, and flat subject–predicate–object summaries are compact only because they discard the study arms, doses, subgroups, and experimental models that make findings comparable. This study presents the Context–Evidence Graph (CEG), a compact structured record of study findings designed so that a large, heterogeneous literature (or a compact view of it) can sit in a model’s working context at once, with each finding’s arms, effect size and direction, supporting and contradicting evidence, and experimental setting kept intact. The purpose is to let a model explore across studies, look for the variables that explain disagreements, reconcile conflicting results, and propose new hypotheses and concrete next experiments. Exploration at that scale needs a safeguard, because a fluent draft can cite real studies and quote real numbers while attaching a number to the wrong outcome, study arm, unit prefix, or inequality bound, or while carrying a result across an experimental or subgroup gap that the source study does not cover. CEG therefore pairs the representation with deterministic checks that run whenever a synthesis, hypothesis, or correction is written back to the shared graph. This paper formalizes the representation and evaluates that commit-time checking layer, stating what the checks guarantee and where checking of free-text sentences fails; it does not measure how much literature fits in a context window or how often exploration yields discoveries.

#strong[Materials and Methods.] A CEG stores each reported finding as a structured record that links an intervention, population, outcome, measurement, and experimental setting to its source study (an `EvidenceAtom`). Each record names every study arm in the comparison (an explicit multi-arm contrast); states its measurement in one of five mutually exclusive formats, namely an exact value, a relative change, an interval, a censored bound, or a non-numeric outcome (a five-way disjoint-union quantity type); and is placed on a multi-dimensional map of experimental settings, such as mouse in vivo versus human tissue ex vivo versus clinical trial, rather than on a single 1-to-5 evidence ladder (a coordinate in a finite product partial order of contexts). Each cross-study conclusion is a claim that cites both the findings that support it and the findings that contradict it, and a proposal to carry a result across an experimental gap must name the follow-up experiment that would test it. Corrections made after extraction are kept in a reversible, checksum-verified edit log over exact-decimal JSON records (a state-guarded, invertible `JSONPatch` ledger, `RFC 6901`/`RFC 6902`, over canonicalized float-free JSON, `RFC 8785`). When anything is committed to the shared graph, a two-layer verifier checks six invariants, each computable in polynomial time: every reference resolves and every record matches the curated corpus in both directions (referential closure and bidirectional atom-to-corpus binding, $I_1$); the edit log replays forward and backward (bijective ledger replay, $I_2$); every stated number, unit, arm, and direction is grounded at three levels, namely Unicode-hardened lexical tokens ($I_3^(upright("tok"))$), depth-0 per-clause contrast and polarity rules ($I_3^(upright("clause"))$), and exact seven-coordinate typed propositions ($I_3^(upright("typed"))$); results move between experimental settings only where the declared ordering allows (partial-order transfer gating, $I_4$); nothing is promoted directly from an undigitized figure or an unverified source (a primary-source digitization gate, $I_5$); and supporting and counter-evidence stay disjoint, with claim attestations and locks on non-prose metadata ($I_6$). The study evaluates a zero-dependency Python reference implementation across exhaustive finite-domain self-tests, a constructive synthetic benchmark of 1,931 cases (1,521 adversarial, 410 valid) from 199 distinct templates—including 43 sentences from an independent adversarial pass and 48 gauntlet probes—and a primary-source evaluation on 12 peer-reviewed PubMed studies (31 `EvidenceAtom`s) spanning idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, heart failure, and thoracic oncology. All of these experiments test the commit-time checks. A worked example (§5) carries ten findings from eight peer-reviewed studies on islet replacement for type 1 diabetes through extraction, classification against a clinical target setting, logged correction, and the decision on each candidate claim. A case study then applies the representation and the checks to a 12 November 2026 snapshot of the production corpus those studies come from, of 1,151 type 1 diabetes studies (§6.9): it reports the snapshot’s size and structure and replays its 106-event curation ledger through the reference verifier with tamper controls.

#strong[Results.] In brief, the formal guarantees held on every exhaustive test; the full verifier rejected every adversarial case in the core, stress, red-team, and gauntlet suites and in the real-text synthesis track; and the free-text lint showed its two predicted limits, namely wording errors it cannot catch and true sentences it refuses. In detail: (i) State-guarded ledger replay is deterministic, invertible, and tamper-evident (Proposition 1; 182 tampers tried across 15 kinds, 0 accepted), while Layer-1 structure-only verification (`check_top_digests=False`) rejects 26/26 structural tampers without consulting any precomputed digest. (ii) Direct quantitative promotion is sound relative to the declared context partial order, and no scalar evidence ranking is both sound and complete once incomparable models exist (Theorem 1; 0 disagreements across 26,344 ordered context pairs, and 0 faithful scalar rankings among 46,656 assignments). (iii) Any surface prose verifier reading only citations, numerals, units, and polarity cues returns identical verdicts on a true pooled-citation sentence and its endpoint-misbound twin (Theorem 2; Corollary 2.1 verified across 242/242 canonical renderings). (iv) Typed proposition verification accepts a unique proposition per atom up to arm-set ordering and ASCII case (Theorem 3; 133/133 single-coordinate mutants rejected). Across synthetic suites, the full `V3` verifier rejects 600/600 core, 600/600 stress, 43/43 red-team, and 38/38 gauntlet adversarial cases while accepting 200/200 core and 10/10 gauntlet valid controls, and rejects 1,161/1,161 record-level adversarial controls under a Mode B self-attesting adversary that recomputes claim attestations after tampering (60/60 non-prose metadata tampers rejected). On stress valid controls it accepts 100/200 (refusing pooled citations and unmodeled derived or dispersion numerals), and on the residual open-class prose suite it rejects 20/240 (catching pooled `R1` headers while leaving 220/240 single-tuple endpoint renamings and unlisted lexical inversions open as predicted). On the 12-study PubMed evaluation, `V3` accepts 6/36 and refuses 30/36 verbatim abstract sentences (quantifying the rewrite cost imposed by inline confidence intervals, hazard ratios, event denominators, and publisher thin spaces), while accepting 24/24 valid grounded synthesis records and rejecting 60/60 adversarial clinical synthesis defects in both normal and Mode B self-attesting evaluation. At production scale (1,151 studies, 14,105 outcomes), the reference verifier accepts the complete 106-event curation ledger and rejects 5/5 structural tampers of it without consulting any digest; in this snapshot none of the outcomes is certified against its primary source, and the settled corpus occupies 20,412,758 bytes of canonical JSON (§6.9).

#strong[Conclusion.] A CEG is a way to put a large structured literature in front of a language model at once and let it explore freely, while a deterministic gate checks what is written back. The gate gives exact guarantees for structured (typed) claims, for reversible edit logs, and for transfers between experimental settings on the declared context map, which is where swapped arms, unit mix-ups, and unstated leaps such as mouse-to-human are stopped. Free-text sentences are only linted, conservatively, with measured blind spots and a measured rewrite cost on raw clinical prose, so decisions should rest on the typed claims. The experiments establish this integrity layer as a foundation for discovery workflows. A production corpus of 1,151 studies shows the representation and its correction log operating at scale, and shows what remained open at the time of the snapshot: its outcomes were not certified against primary sources, and its full serialization is of the order of millions of tokens. How well exploration over a CEG generates sound hypotheses remains to be evaluated.

#strong[Keywords:] AI for science; hypothesis generation; in-context multi-study synthesis; evidence graphs; translational research; claim verification; provenance

]
== 1. Introduction
<introduction>
Scientific breakthroughs often come from seeing patterns, boundary conditions, and contradictions across hundreds or thousands of studies at once: an effect that appears only at one disease stage, a treatment that works in mice but not in human tissue, two trials that disagree because they enrolled different patients. Large language models (LLMs) can reason over whatever is placed in their context window, but a literature rarely fits there in a usable form. Full papers are long, so even a modest collection exceeds or clutters the window, while flat knowledge-graph triples of the form subject–predicate–object are compact but strip away the study arms, doses, subgroups, and experimental models that make two findings comparable. Context–Evidence Graphs (CEGs) are designed to sit between these extremes. Quantitative evidence in biomedicine and engineering comes from heterogeneous sources: cell assays, animal disease models, explanted human tissue, subgroup analyses, and randomized clinical trials. A CEG condenses each reported finding into a small structured record that keeps its study arms, measurement unit, statistic, sample size, and experimental context, and it records each cross-study conclusion together with the findings that support it and the findings that contradict it. The aim is that a large, heterogeneous body of evidence, or a compact view of it, can be loaded into a model’s working context at once, so that the model can compare results across studies, look for the variables that explain disagreements, and propose new hypotheses together with the experiments that would test them.

To see both the opportunity and the hazard in plain terms, consider synthesizing thousands of studies on preserving or replacing insulin-producing beta cells in type 1 diabetes. Across that literature, candidate therapies routinely reverse high blood glucose in newly diabetic mice, whereas experiments on donated human pancreatic islets kept alive in culture test genuine human cells without a bloodstream or an active autoimmune attack, and clinical trials span newly diagnosed children who retain residual insulin production through adults with longstanding disease receiving systemic immunosuppression. Distilling each paper into compact, structured study arms, doses, patient groups, follow-up windows, and measured outcomes allows a language model to hold thousands of structured findings in working memory at once—comparing results across an entire literature to notice, for example, whether an intervention succeeds only before beta-cell loss is complete or reverses direction under a particular immunosuppressive regimen. Yet at that scale, a fluent synthesis that swaps active and control C-peptide values, confuses a micromolar culture concentration with a clinical dose, or presents mouse reversal as established human clinical efficacy states a substantive falsehood while citing real papers and real numbers. (§5 works through ten findings from eight peer-reviewed studies on exactly this problem, §6 evaluates the framework formally and empirically on synthetic benchmarks and 12 peer-reviewed clinical and preclinical studies across idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, heart failure, and thoracic oncology, and §6.9 reports the production corpus of 1,151 type 1 diabetes studies from which §5’s findings are drawn.)

#strong[Why large-scale in-context discovery needs a deterministic commit gate.] Exploration at this scale produces drafts: syntheses, hypotheses, and corrections that a model proposes to write back to the shared graph. LLMs and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipelines draft fluent quantitative syntheses rapidly \[1–3\], and their surface citations and numerals often appear genuine: the cited papers exist and the quoted numbers occur somewhere in those papers. Auditing whether each number remains bound to the right outcome, arm, unit scale, inequality direction, and target population is slow, and automated factuality evaluators largely leave that structural binding unchecked. If such drafts are saved unchecked, each error becomes part of the evidence that the next round of exploration reads. The characteristic errors recombine true material from cited papers rather than inventing citations outright:

+ #strong[Mixing numbers across outcomes or across cited studies.] A study reports a target biomarker reduction ($29.4 med upright("ng/mL")$) alongside a slice viability control ($96.2 %$), or one sentence cites three trials in sequence, clause by clause. Attaching $96.2$ to the biomarker, rotating values among the three cited clauses ($A arrow.r B arrow.r C arrow.r A$), or copying one clause’s number into a second clause while keeping it in the first all pass a check that only asks whether each number appears somewhere in the cited study or sentence (a study-wide or sentence-wide bag-of-numbers check).
+ #strong[Swapping treatment and control arms or misreading negated bounds.] The pair of numbers $\(29.4\,48.6\)$ is kept but assigned to the wrong arms, so the treatment value is reported for the comparator and the comparator value for the treatment. The same swap can hide inside a comma-separated aside (an appositive), as in #emph[“the placebo arm had a change, $- 28.5 med upright("mL")$, compared with treatment”], where the number between the commas belongs to the treatment arm but reads as the placebo’s. Or a negated bound is read without flipping it: `not < 5%` means at least 5%, and `never > 28 days` means at most 28 days.
+ #strong[Confusing look-alike units, symbols, or figure labels.] Simple pattern-matching number extractors (regular expressions) miss numbers written as words (#emph[tripled], #emph[sixty-four percent]) and as fraction characters (vulgar fractions); are fooled by invisible or combining Unicode characters that split a number (`U+0301`, `U+20DD`) and by Cyrillic or Greek letters that look identical to Latin ones (homoglyphs) \[30, 45\]; merge micromolar concentration (`uM`, `mM`, `nM`) with micrometre or millimetre length (`um`, `mm`, `nm`) when letter case is ignored; or mistake a figure panel label (`Fig. 28 d`) for a duration (`days`).
+ #strong[Confusing relative percentages with raw values, or dropping third study arms.] Forcing every outcome into two slots, `(treatment, control)`, turns a $72.0 %$ relative reduction into what looks like a measured level and silently drops the active comparator arm of a three-arm study.
+ #strong[Flattening different experimental models onto a single ranking ladder.] Evidence hierarchies with a single axis rank study designs on one ordinal scale \[4\]. Translational and clinical contexts vary along multiple axes simultaneously. A murine bleomycin model provides systemic pharmacokinetics and immune cell recruitment, whereas human precision-cut lung slices (PCLS) preserve aged human target biology and fibrotic matrix ex vivo without systemic circulation \[5, 6\]; similarly, a stage II–IIIA resected lung cancer primary analysis and an overall stage IB–IIIA population differ along a disease-stage axis even within one Phase 3 trial \[43\]. Contexts that cannot be ordered against each other (incomparable contexts) admit no single scalar ranking without either discarding complementary evidence or licensing unsupported extrapolation.
+ #strong[Unrecorded post-extraction edits or unverified claims tucked into metadata.] Raw literature extractions need repair after the fact: fixing case-folded units, restoring dropped comparator arms, correcting subgroup sample sizes, or flagging Kaplan–Meier estimates read off a figure that has not been digitized. Without a reversible, checksum-guarded edit log (an invertible, digest-guarded patch ledger) and locks over the metadata fields that are not prose, edits cannot be traced back to the raw extraction, and unverified statements can be placed in fields that no check reads.

CEGs address these failure classes with one representation and one set of deterministic checks: the structured records that the model explores are the same records that the checks run against (formally, a typed hypergraph with a deterministic verification pipeline). The central design principle is a strict separation between #strong[exact structural guarantees] and #strong[heuristic prose linting]. A free-text sentence cannot be tied to one piece of evidence just by inspecting its surface citations, numerals, units, and polarity cues; Theorem 2 proves an exact indistinguishability barrier for that class of verifiers. CEGs therefore place every decision-bearing claim in a typed proposition, a structured statement that is compared field by field, on seven coordinates, with its `EvidenceAtom` (Theorem 3); govern movement between experimental settings by a product partial order (Theorem 1); track corrections in an edit log that replays in both directions (bijective `JSONPatch` replay, Proposition 1); and pair these exact layers with a conservative prose lint whose false-negative boundaries and verbatim clinical rewrite cost are measured directly in §6.

#strong[Design intent: explore free in context, verify at commit.] CEGs are meant to be used in two regimes, and the distinction governs how every result in this paper should be read. #emph[Regime 1 (in-context exploration and hypothesis discovery) is the primary operating mode.] Condensing heterogeneous studies into compact `EvidenceAtom`s, with their study-arm variables, effect magnitudes and directions, and positions on the multi-axis map of experimental settings (`ContextSignature` coordinates), lets an entire multi-study outcome network sit inside a language model’s context window at once. While exploring, synthesizing, and generating hypotheses, the model is intended to traverse that network freely in natural language, under no prose-lint restriction: spotting cross-study patterns, looking for moderating variables where effects differ between a claim’s supporting and counter-evidence findings ($T^(+)$ versus $T^(-)$), proposing cross-model hypotheses, and drafting candidate graph additions or curation edits.

#emph[Regime 2 (commit-time verification) is its guardrail.] Verification under $I_1$–$I_6$ sits only at the persistence and promotion boundary. It checks what is committed back to the shared graph: claim records (`SynthesisClaimRecord`s), their structured field-by-field statements (`TypedProposition`s) and evidence-anchor sentences (`prose_sentences`), and curation edits (`ReversiblePatchEvent`s). At that boundary $I_3$ catches swapped numbers, arms, and units, and $I_4$ sorts cross-setting claims into three kinds. A result whose source setting already covers the target (`DIRECT_MATCH`) may be promoted directly. A hypothesis that bridges a gap between comparable settings (a `CANDIDATE_UPGRADE` over a `BOUNDED_BRIDGE`) must carry a concrete follow-up experiment that would test every unresolved experimental gap (a `ProspectiveFalsifierGate` covering every unresolved axis). An explanation in terms of a moderating variable is recorded as a `SCOPING_QUALIFICATION`. The gate does more than tag cross-context claims: a `SUPPORTS` or `CANDIDATE_UPGRADE` record is rejected with `I4_INADMISSIBLE_CONTEXT_TRANSFER` whenever any of its supporting tuples is an `INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER` (§4.1). The guardrail serves discovery: it keeps the shared graph clean as it grows and leaves exploration itself unrestricted.

This two-regime reading is a statement of design intent, not an empirical result. This paper formalizes the CEG representation and empirically benchmarks its Regime 2 integrity and transfer layer as the foundation for large-scale discovery workflows: every experiment in §6 evaluates the commit gate (Regime 2) only, and no experiment in this paper measures how much literature fits in a context window, the quality of upstream exploration, or the rate at which it yields discoveries.

=== Contributions
<contributions>
+ #strong[A compact evidence-graph representation and two-layer verification model (§3–§4).] Evidence atoms with a strict five-way disjoint-union quantity type, truncation-free multi-arm contrasts, colon-safe bibliographic tuple identifiers (`PMID:...`, `DOI:...`), product partial-order transfer gating, invertible state-guarded `JSONPatch` ledgers, non-prose surface digest locks, and two-layer verification separating structure-only closure (`Layer 1`) from cryptographic digest pinning (`Layer 2`).
+ #strong[Formal guarantees and impossibility bounds (§4.3).] Proofs of ledger determinism, invertibility, and tamper evidence (Proposition 1); transfer soundness and scalar-ranking impossibility on incomparable context posets (Theorem 1); surface-verifier indistinguishability on pooled citations and canonical single-tuple completability (Theorem 2 and Corollary 2.1); and unique seven-coordinate exactness of the typed proposition layer (Theorem 3).
+ #strong[Zero-dependency reference implementation, dual-mode synthetic benchmark, and primary-source PubMed evaluation (§5–§6).] A standard-library implementation (`ceg_reference.py` `v3.1`, byte-identical across Python `3.9`–`3.14` under both normal and `-O` optimized execution), exhaustive finite-domain self-tests, a 1,931-case synthetic benchmark evaluated under both raw tamper (`Mode A`) and self-attesting tamper (`Mode B`), and an empirical evaluation on 12 peer-reviewed PubMed studies across idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF), heart failure (HF), and thoracic oncology \[33–44\].
+ #strong[A worked example on real studies (§5) and a production-scale case study (§6.9).] Ten findings from eight peer-reviewed studies on islet replacement for type 1 diabetes, curated into the representation, classified against a clinical target context, and submitted to the gate, with four cross-study hypotheses held behind prospective falsifier gates and four adversarial probes rejected. A dated snapshot of a corpus of 1,151 type 1 diabetes studies (14,105 outcomes) with its size and structure; a replay of its 106-event correction log through the reference verifier, with tamper controls; and a plain account of what that snapshot does not verify (no certified outcomes, a prioritized subset, language-model extraction, and a serialization of the order of millions of tokens).

== 2. Related Work
<related-work>
=== 2.1 Evidence grading, indirectness, and translational validity
<evidence-grading-indirectness-and-translational-validity>
Systematic reviews follow PRISMA reporting standards \[8\] and Cochrane synthesis methodology \[9\], while GRADE evaluates certainty of evidence across risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision, and publication bias \[7\]. Within GRADE, indirectness captures mismatches across population, intervention, comparator, and outcome (PICO) between source studies and target clinical questions \[10\]. Critiques of rigid evidence pyramids emphasize that study design alone does not determine validity \[4\]. In preclinical-to-clinical translation, animal efficacy frequently fails to replicate in human trials \[11, 31, 32\]; American Thoracic Society consensus guidance on pulmonary fibrosis models explicitly notes that murine bleomycin fibrosis resolves spontaneously and recommends complementary confirmation in human fibrotic tissue such as precision-cut lung slices \[5, 6\]. CEGs do not replace statistical meta-analysis or clinical judgment; instead, they encode indirectness formally as a product partial order $\(cal(C)\,scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C))\)$, compute the exact set of unresolved axes $U\(c_s\,c_t\)$, and block quantitative parameter promotion across unresolved or below-floor gaps.

=== 2.2 Computational models of scientific claims, argumentation, and provenance
<computational-models-of-scientific-claims-argumentation-and-provenance>
Computational claim models build on Toulmin’s argument layout \[12\] and Dung’s abstract argumentation frameworks \[13\]. Nanopublications link assertions to provenance graphs \[14\], and micropublications model attribution and support/challenge networks over biomedical statements and data figures \[15\]. W3C `PROV-DM` \[16\] and the FAIR data principles \[17\] standardize lineage metadata, while large biomedical knowledge graphs such as SemMedDB \[18\] and PrimeKG \[19\] aggregate subject–predicate–object triples across literature and ontologies. Because entity–relation triples omit multi-arm contrasts, exact measurement units, sample sizes, and experimental context coordinates, they cannot detect arm swaps, unit-scale folds, or subgroup-to-overall promotions. CEGs operate one level deeper, representing individual study outcomes as typed hypergraph atoms bound to a replayable corpus extraction.

=== 2.3 Automated factuality and table/claim verification
<automated-factuality-and-tableclaim-verification>
Attribution frameworks such as AIS \[1\], FActScore \[2\], and ALCE \[3\] decompose generated text into atomic statements and score support using natural-language inference (NLI) models. In biomedical and scientific verification, SciFact checks single-sentence claims against abstracts \[20\], Evidence Inference classifies intervention–comparator–outcome directions in randomized trial reports \[21\], RobotReviewer extracts risk-of-bias judgments \[22\], and TabFact \[23\] and SciTab \[24\] benchmark compositional reasoning over tables. Neural entailment models handle open-ended lexical paraphrase that deterministic parsers refuse, but their verdicts are probabilistic, model-dependent, and vulnerable to subtle numerical, unit-case, or subgroup swaps. CEG verification is complementary and strictly deterministic: upstream human curators or language models extract structured records and draft claims, after which the CEG verifier enforces exact algebraic, numeric, unit, and context invariants.

=== 2.4 Canonical JSON, document patching, and Unicode security
<canonical-json-document-patching-and-unicode-security>
Post-extraction curation in CEGs builds on IETF `RFC 6901` JSON Pointer \[25\], a state-guarded restriction of `RFC 6902` JSON Patch \[26\], and `RFC 8785` JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS) \[27\] restricted to float-free trees so decimal quantities never pass through IEEE-754 binary floating-point conversion. Lexical normalization and homoglyph defenses draw on Unicode Technical Report \#36 (#emph[Unicode Security Considerations], Revision 15) \[30\] and its active normative companion, Unicode Technical Standard \#39 (#emph[Unicode Security Mechanisms], Version 18.0.0) \[45\].

== 3. Formal Model
<formal-model>
Informally, a CEG represents each reported study finding as a self-contained, unit-aware building block (`EvidenceAtom`), locates its experimental setting on a multi-axis map of contexts rather than a single ranking ladder, records every post-extraction correction as a reversible edit, and represents each cross-study conclusion as a hyperedge connecting supporting and opposing findings. Three of these pieces do more than block errors. A claim’s supporting and counter-evidence anchors ($T^(+)$ and $T^(-)$) record where studies disagree, which is how the graph represents scientific anomalies. The multi-axis context map (`ContextSignature`) records the setting in which each finding holds, which is how it represents boundary conditions. The `ProspectiveFalsifierGate` records a concrete next experiment (which arms to compare head to head, on which readout, against what pass threshold) for testing a hypothesis that crosses experimental models. Formally, all CEG structures are finite and serialize to canonical float-free JSON (`RFC 8785`, rejecting `NaN` and `±Infinity` on parse). Continuous and discrete measurement magnitudes are stored as exact ASCII decimal strings and compared via arbitrary-precision `Decimal` arithmetic.

#strong[Key terms in plain language.] The definitions below use the following names, which are also the names used in the reference implementation.

- #strong[`EvidenceAtom`]: one reported finding from one study, stored with its arms, numbers, unit, statistic, sample size, experimental setting, and a pointer to the source passage.
- #strong[`ContextSignature`]: the experimental setting of a finding or of a claim, given as one value on each axis of the context map (for example model system, disease stage, dosing, and endpoint).
- #strong[Transfer class]: the answer to the question of whether a finding from one setting can speak to another. It is `DIRECT_MATCH` when the source setting covers the target, `BOUNDED_BRIDGE` when a gap remains that a named experiment could close, and `INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER` when the gap cannot be bridged.
- #strong[`SynthesisClaimRecord`]: one cross-study conclusion or hypothesis, together with the findings that support it ($T^(+)$) and the findings that count against it ($T^(-)$).
- #strong[`TypedProposition`]: the structured, field-by-field form of what a claim states about one finding. The exact checks compare this form.
- #strong[Evidence-anchor sentences (`prose_sentences`)]: the free-text sentences attached to a claim for human readers. They are linted conservatively and are not verified exactly.
- #strong[`ProspectiveFalsifierGate`]: the follow-up experiment attached to a bridging hypothesis, naming the arms to compare, the readout, and the pass threshold.
- #strong[`ReversiblePatchEvent`]: one logged correction to the extracted data, which can be replayed forward or undone.
- #strong[Invariants $I_1$–$I_6$ and the two layers]: the six conditions that a commit must satisfy (§4.1). Layer 1 checks the content itself, and Layer 2 adds comparisons against stored checksums.

=== 3.1 Product context partial order and transfer classifier
<product-context-partial-order-and-transfer-classifier>
Let $D = { 1\,dots.h\,d }$ index experimental or clinical validity axes (for example, #emph[model system], #emph[disease chronicity or stage], #emph[dosing and exposure], and #emph[endpoint maturity]).

#emph[In plain terms:] each experimental setting is a point on several independent axes (for example model system, disease stage, dosing, and endpoint); a result may be carried directly from one setting to another only when the source setting covers the target on every axis, and otherwise the definition says exactly which axes remain open and whether the gap can be bridged at all.

#strong[Definition 1 (product context poset and transfer classifier).] For each axis $k in D$, let $\(cal(C)_k\,scripts(prec.curly.eq)_k\)$ be a finite partially ordered set (reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive), where $x scripts(prec.curly.eq)_k y$ encodes the explicit domain judgment that condition $y$ dominates or covers condition $x$ toward deployment validity on axis $k$. Incomparable elements $x scripts(parallel)_k y$ represent complementary experimental models or non-nested patient strata. The product context space is

$ cal(C) = product_(k = 1)^d cal(C)_k\,#h(2em) c scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c' arrow.l.r.double forall k in D : med c_k scripts(prec.curly.eq)_k c'_k . $

Given a source context $c_s in cal(C)$ and a target context $c_t in cal(C)$, the #emph[unresolved axis set] is

$ U\(c_s\,c_t\)= { thin k in D : c_(t\,k) scripts(prec.curly.eq.not)_k c_(s\,k) thin } . $

Elements $x\,y in cal(C)_k$ have a #emph[common upper bound], written $upright("cub")_k\(x\,y\)$, iff $exists z in cal(C)_k$ such that $x scripts(prec.curly.eq)_k z$ and $y scripts(prec.curly.eq)_k z$. Let $b : B arrow.r union.big_(k in B) cal(C)_k$ assign to a subset of axes $B subset.eq D$ a minimum #emph[bridge floor] $b\(k\)in cal(C)_k$. The three-way transfer classifier $tau_b : cal(C) times cal(C) arrow.r { tt("DIRECT_MATCH")\,tt("BOUNDED_BRIDGE")\,tt("INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER") }$ is

$ tau_b\(c_s\,c_t\)= cases(delim: "{", tt("DIRECT_MATCH") & upright("if ") U\(c_s\,c_t\)= nothing\,, tt("INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER") & upright("if ") exists k in U\(c_s\,c_t\): med not thin upright("cub")_k\(c_(s\,k)\,c_(t\,k)\)med or med #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\($] k in B and b\(k\)scripts(prec.curly.eq.not)_k c_(s\,k) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\)$]\,, tt("BOUNDED_BRIDGE") & upright("otherwise.")) $

`DIRECT_MATCH` holds iff $c_t scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c_s$ on every axis. `BOUNDED_BRIDGE` holds when one or more axes remain unresolved ($U eq.not nothing$), yet on every unresolved axis $c_(s\,k)$ shares a common upper bound with $c_(t\,k)$ and clears any declared bridge floor $b\(k\)$, permitting conditional synthesis behind an explicit prospective falsifier gate. `INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER` holds whenever an unresolved axis lies below its bridge floor or on a disconnected branch with no common upper bound. Poset axioms and floor membership are checked algorithmically as part of graph integrity ($I_1$).

