=== 3.2 Disjoint-union quantities, multi-arm contrasts, and colon-safe evidence atoms
<disjoint-union-quantities-multi-arm-contrasts-and-colon-safe-evidence-atoms>
#emph[In plain terms:] every measurement is written in exactly one of five formats (a value with its unit, a relative change with its direction, an interval, a bound such as more than 28 days, or an explicit statement that no number is available), and the fields of the other four formats must be empty, so a percentage change can never be mistaken for a measured level.

#strong[Definition 2 (strict disjoint-union quantity type).] Every quantitative slot takes a value in the five-variant disjoint union $cal(Q) = cal(Q)_(upright("level")) union.sq cal(Q)_(upright("rel")) union.sq cal(Q)_(upright("int")) union.sq cal(Q)_(upright("cens")) union.sq cal(Q)_(upright("miss"))$, where every inactive variant’s fields must be `None` and every literal $v$ must be a canonical ASCII decimal matching `-?(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*)(?:\.[0-9]+)?` in full (an optional minus sign, no plus sign, no leading zero, no exponent, ASCII digits only):

- #strong[`point_level`]: $\(rho\,v\,u\,d_u\)$ with $rho in { =\,approx }$, non-empty unit $u$, non-empty unit dimension $d_u$, and `lower = upper = change_direction = missing_reason = None`.
- #strong[`relative_change`]: $\(rho\,v\,u\,delta\)$ with $rho$ any of the six relation operators of `RELATION_OPS` (`=`, `≈`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`), $u in { %\,tt("fold")\,tt("ratio")\,tt("log2_fc") }$, mandatory direction $delta in { tt("increase")\,tt("decrease")\,tt("neutral") }$, and `lower = upper = missing_reason = None`.
- #strong[`interval_bound`]: $\(\[v_(upright("lo"))\,v_(upright("hi"))\]\,u\,d_u\)$ with $v_(upright("lo")) lt.eq v_(upright("hi"))$ as exact `Decimal`s, non-empty $u$ and $d_u$, and `relation = value = change_direction = missing_reason = None`.
- #strong[`censored_observation`]: $\(rho\,v\,u\)$ with strict inequality or bound operator $rho in { >\,gt.eq\,<\,lt.eq }$, non-empty $u$, and `lower = upper = change_direction = missing_reason = None`.
- #strong[`missing_numeric`]: $\(tt("missing_reason")\)$ with non-empty reason string and `relation = value = lower = upper = unit = unit_dimension = change_direction = None`.

#emph[In plain terms:] a comparison must list every study arm that the source reports for that outcome, so the third arm of a three-arm trial cannot be silently dropped, and an `EvidenceAtom` bundles one outcome’s comparison, values, statistic, sample size, experimental setting, and pointer back to the source passage into a single record.

#strong[Definition 3 (truncation-free multi-arm contrast and `EvidenceAtom`).] Let $cal(V)\(s\)$ denote the declared arm variables of study $s$, and $upright("Mentions")\(t\)subset.eq cal(V)\(s\)$ the arm variables named in the primary source for outcome tuple $t$. A #emph[multi-arm contrast] $gamma =\(A_(upright("focal"))\,A_(upright("comp"))\,kappa\)$ over duplicate-free tuples of arm IDs satisfies: (1) $A_(upright("focal")) eq.not nothing$, $A_(upright("focal")) union A_(upright("comp")) subset.eq cal(V)\(s\)$, and $A_(upright("focal")) inter A_(upright("comp")) = nothing$\; (2) cardinality agreement with $kappa in { tt("single_arm")\,tt("pairwise")\,tt("multi_arm_joint") }$ ($\(\|A_(upright("focal"))\|\,\|A_(upright("comp"))\|\)=\(1\,0\)$ for `single_arm`, $\(1\,1\)$ for `pairwise`, and $\|A_(upright("focal"))\|+\|A_(upright("comp"))\|gt.eq 3$ for `multi_arm_joint`); and (3) #emph[non-truncation]: $upright("Mentions")\(t\)subset.eq A_(upright("focal")) union A_(upright("comp"))$.

Outcome tuple identifiers take the right-anchored form $t = s : o$, partitioned at the #strong[rightmost] colon (`split_outcome_tuple`, `rsplit(":", 1)`), where the outcome local identifier $o$ is the non-empty remainder and therefore contains no colon; to be recognized as a citation in prose (`TUPLE_RE`) it must have the form `O\d+[A-Za-z0-9_-]*` (`O1`, `O2_itt`). The study/document prefix $s$ may carry standard bibliographic namespace colons and slashes (`S104`, `PMID:24836310`, `DOI:10.1056/NEJMoa1402582`). An `EvidenceAtom` is the 13-tuple

$ a = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\($] tt("ATOM:") t\,med t\,med s\,med upright("ep")\,med c_a\,med gamma\,med q_(upright("focal"))\,med q_(upright("comp"))\,med upright("stat")\,med n\,med p_(upright("int"))\,med pi\,med upright("prov") #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\)$]\, $

with balanced-bracket endpoint name $upright("ep")$ free of forbidden Unicode, context signature $c_a in cal(C)$, $q_(upright("focal")) in cal(Q)$, $q_(upright("comp")) in cal(Q) union { tack.t }$, statistic kind $upright("stat")$ from the closed vocabulary `STATISTIC_KINDS` (`mean`, `median`, `proportion`, `rate`, `rate_ratio`, `hazard_ratio`, `odds_ratio`, `difference`, `fold_change`, `qualitative`), non-negative integer sample size $n in bb(Z)_(gt.eq 0) union { tack.t }$ (`bool` and `float` rejected), optional interval/$p$-value string $p_(upright("int"))$, closed polarity $pi in { tt("favorable")\,tt("unfavorable")\,tt("neutral_or_mixed")\,tt("unresolved") }$ (`ATOM_POLARITIES`), and `ProvenancePin` $upright("prov") =\(upright("doc_id")\,upright("loc")\,upright("quote")\,tt("requires_figure_digitization")\,tt("primary_pdf_verified")\)$. Qualitative outcomes require `missing_numeric`, and outcomes with `requires_figure_digitization = True` cannot carry a `point_level` quantity at all (neither $=$ nor $approx$).

=== 3.3 Hypergraph claims, non-prose surface locks, and bijective patch ledger
<hypergraph-claims-non-prose-surface-locks-and-bijective-patch-ledger>
#emph[In plain terms:] the graph consists of the context map, the studies, their arms, the findings, and the claims; each claim is a record that points to the findings supporting it and the findings against it, says what kind of claim it is and which setting it is about, and carries both its free-text sentences and its field-by-field structured statements.

#strong[Definition 4 (Context–Evidence Graph and synthesis claim hyperedge).] A CEG is $cal(G) =\(Lambda\,cal(S)\,cal(V)\,cal(A)\,cal(H)\)$. Each synthesis claim $h in cal(H)$ is a directed hyperedge

$ h = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\($] upright("id")\,med omega\,med c_(upright("target"))\,med T^(+)\,med T^(-)\,med Sigma_(upright("prose"))\,med cal(P)_(upright("typed"))\,med g_(upright("prosp"))\,med upright("prom")\,med alpha #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\)$]\, $

connecting supporting tuples $T^(+)$ and counter-evidence tuples $T^(-)$ to a claim with closed epistemic polarity $omega in { tt("SUPPORTS")\,tt("CONTRADICTS")\,tt("SCOPING_QUALIFICATION")\,tt("CANDIDATE_UPGRADE")\,tt("FALSIFIED_GRAVEYARD") }$ at target context $c_(upright("target")) in cal(C)$, prose sentences $Sigma_(upright("prose"))$, typed propositions $cal(P)_(upright("typed"))$, optional `ProspectiveFalsifierGate` $g_(upright("prosp"))$, promotion request flag $upright("prom") in { upright("true")\,upright("false") }$, and optional deterministic claim-attestation digest $alpha$ (the field `attestation_sha256`, computed by `compute_claim_attestation_sha256` over the record’s structured fields, cited tuples, and extracted numeric mentions). These ten components are the ten fields of `SynthesisClaimRecord`. When a record is serialized as an envelope (`record_to_envelope`), every key outside `ACTIVE_PROSE_KEYS = {"prose_sentences"}` is canonicalized and bound into the non-prose surface digest $H_(upright("meta")) = H\(h\\Sigma_(upright("prose"))\)$ (`non_prose_surface_sha256`). $H_(upright("meta"))$ is not a field of the record: it is held beside the record and supplied to the verifier as the expected value, so unverified claims cannot be smuggled inside auxiliary metadata strings.

#emph[In plain terms:] every correction made after extraction is logged as one small edit to one study’s record, with the old value, the new value, a reason, and a checksum of that study’s record before and after, so the whole history can be replayed forward to the curated data or backward to the raw extraction.

#strong[Definition 5 (state-guarded bijective `JSONPatch` ledger).] Let $D_0$ be the raw extracted corpus JSON and $D_m$ the settled corpus JSON. A curation ledger $cal(L) =\(e_1\,dots.h\,e_m\)$ is a sequence of `ReversiblePatchEvent`s

$ e_i =\(upright("id")_i\,med s_i\,med upright("ptr")_i\,med v_i^(upright("old"))\,med v_i^(upright("new"))\,med upright("rat")_i\,med H_i^(upright("pre"))\,med H_i^(upright("post"))\) $

satisfying: (a) $upright("ptr")_i$ is a canonical `RFC 6901` JSON Pointer (array indices match `0` or `[1-9][0-9]*`, forbidding `-1` or leading-zero aliases) addressing an existing mutable target strictly inside study $s_i$’s record and never altering `study_id`\; (b) $v_i^(upright("old")) eq.not v_i^(upright("new"))$ under canonical `RFC 8785` encoding (forbidding no-op padding); (c) event IDs $upright("id")_i$ are pairwise distinct, and each event’s identifier, study identifier, pointer, and rationale carry no forbidden Unicode (`I2_FORBIDDEN_UNICODE_IN_LEDGER_STRING`); and (d) $H_i^(upright("pre"))$ and $H_i^(upright("post"))$ equal the `RFC 8785` SHA-256 digests of study $s_i$’s isolated JSON subtree immediately before and after replacing $v_i^(upright("old"))$ with $v_i^(upright("new"))$.

== 4. Verification Invariants, Formal Guarantees, and Prose Lint Boundaries
<verification-invariants-formal-guarantees-and-prose-lint-boundaries>
The checks in this section run only when something is committed to the shared graph (Regime 2). They exist to keep that graph trustworthy as exploration adds to it, and two of them also preserve what exploration found: $I_4$ requires a hypothesis that crosses experimental models to carry its `ProspectiveFalsifierGate`, the concrete experiment that would test it, and $I_6$ keeps a claim’s supporting and counter-evidence anchors ($T^(+)$ and $T^(-)$) disjoint, so recorded disagreements between studies stay visible instead of being merged away.

=== 4.1 Six invariants and two-layer package verification
<six-invariants-and-two-layer-package-verification>
#emph[In plain terms:] a commit is accepted only if six conditions all hold. Every reference points to something that exists, and the graph matches the curated data exactly ($I_1$). The edit log replays forward and backward ($I_2$). Every number, unit, arm, and direction in the claim matches the finding it cites ($I_3$). A result is carried to another experimental setting only where the context map allows, and a bridging hypothesis names the experiment that would test it ($I_4$). Nothing is promoted from a figure that has not been digitized or from a source that has not been verified ($I_5$). Evidence for and against a claim stays separate, and no unchecked text can hide in metadata ($I_6$).

A CEG package and synthesis claim pass verification iff all six invariants hold:

- #strong[$I_1$ (graph integrity, referential closure, and bidirectional corpus binding).] $Lambda$ satisfies poset axioms; every context coordinate in $cal(S)$, $cal(A)$, and $c_(upright("target"))$ belongs to $Lambda$\; every `EvidenceAtom` satisfies Definitions 2–3, with no forbidden Unicode in its identifier, endpoint, unit, arm-identifier, interval/$p$-value, and provenance strings, balanced brackets in its endpoint name and units, and no confusable homoglyph in its endpoint name, units, and arm identifiers; every anchor tuple in $T^(+) union T^(-)$ resolves in $cal(A)$ (`I1_DANGLING_ANCHOR_TUPLE`), and every tuple cited in $Sigma_(upright("prose"))$ or $cal(P)_(upright("typed"))$ belongs to the record’s declared anchor set $T^(+) union T^(-)$ (`I1_UNDECLARED_PROSE_TUPLE`, `I1_UNDECLARED_PROPOSITION_TUPLE`); record and proposition identifiers carry no forbidden Unicode (`I1_FORBIDDEN_UNICODE_IN_RECORD_ID`, `I1_FORBIDDEN_UNICODE_IN_PROPOSITION_ID`); and `verify_atoms_bound_to_corpus` confirms #strong[bidirectional parity] between $cal(G)$ and $D_m$: no orphan study or outcome exists on either side (`I1_STUDY_WITHOUT_CORPUS_RECORD`, `I1_CORPUS_STUDY_WITHOUT_GRAPH_RECORD`, including zero-outcome corpus entries), and every field recorded in $D_m$ (endpoint, quantity kind, relation, exact value/interval bounds, unit, change direction, focal/comparator arms, statistic kind, sample size, and provenance flags) agrees exactly with $cal(A)$. Provenance flags must be genuine booleans: `EvidenceAtom.validate` refuses any other type (`PROVENANCE_BOOLEAN_MUST_BE_BOOL`, reported as `I1_ATOM_VALIDATION_ERROR`), the corpus comparison refuses a non-boolean flag in $D_m$ (`I1_ATOM_CORPUS_FIELD_MISMATCH`), and the claim check refuses a cited atom whose flag is not a boolean (`I1_INVALID_PROVENANCE_BOOLEAN`).
- #strong[$I_2$ (bijective ledger replay).] Forward replay of $cal(L)$ from $D_0$ satisfies all per-step pre/post study digests and old-value guards to produce $D_m$, and reverse replay of $e_m^(- 1)\,dots.h\,e_1^(- 1)$ from $D_m$ reproduces $D_0$.
- #strong[$I_3$ (three-tier proposition grounding).]
  - #strong[$I_3^(upright("tok"))$] (Unicode-hardened lexical & role-typed token grounding): prose contains no forbidden invisible, combining/enclosing, non-ASCII space, or confusable-script characters; no non-canonical numeral evasions; and every extracted numeric/unit mention is licensed by its scoped atom(s) in a role consistent with its unit and SI prefix case (§4.2).
  - #strong[$I_3^(upright("clause"))$] (per-clause contrast, arm-order, inequality, and polarity lint): every depth-0 clause carrying tuple citations grounds its mentions in #emph[that clause’s] cited tuple pool (rejecting pooled multi-tuple numeral clauses under strict mode), enforces conservative contrast-connector requirements on multi-arm clauses, and checks arm attribution, negated inequality inversion, and directional polarity cues (§4.2).
  - #strong[$I_3^(upright("typed"))$] (exact seven-coordinate proposition homomorphism): every $p in cal(P)_(upright("typed"))$ matches its target atom on all seven coordinates with duplicate-free arm tuples (Theorem 3), and $upright("prom") = upright("true")$ requires $cal(P)_(upright("typed"))$ to cover every $t in T^(+)$.
- #strong[$I_4$ (partial-order transfer and falsifier-gate completeness).] Positive claims ($omega in { tt("SUPPORTS")\,tt("CANDIDATE_UPGRADE") }$) require $T^(+) eq.not nothing$ and forbid $tau_b\(c_a\,c_(upright("target"))\)= tt("INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER")$ on any $t in T^(+)$. Direct promotion ($upright("prom") = upright("true")$) requires $omega = tt("SUPPORTS")$ and $tau_b\(c_a\,c_(upright("target"))\)= tt("DIRECT_MATCH")$ for every $t in T^(+)$. Whenever $U_union = union.big_(t in T^(+)) U\(c_a\,c_(upright("target"))\)eq.not nothing$ on a `CANDIDATE_UPGRADE` (or whenever a `ProspectiveFalsifierGate` is attached), the gate must name only declared axes of $Lambda$, cover $U_union$, specify two distinct non-empty head-to-head arms, and state a non-empty primary readout with unit and a non-empty quantitative pass threshold (the gate carries one threshold field, `quantitative_pass_threshold`). The three failure codes are `I4_MISSING_PROSPECTIVE_FALSIFIER_GATE` (no gate on a `CANDIDATE_UPGRADE` with $U_union eq.not nothing$), `I4_INCOMPLETE_PROSPECTIVE_FALSIFIER_AXES` (the gate does not cover $U_union$), and `I4_INVALID_PROSPECTIVE_FALSIFIER_GATE` (a malformed gate). A non-promoting `SUPPORTS` record over a `BOUNDED_BRIDGE` needs no gate and receives the terminal decision `QUALITATIVE_SCOPING_ONLY`\; transfer is classified over $T^(+)$ only, so `CONTRADICTS`, `SCOPING_QUALIFICATION`, and `FALSIFIED_GRAVEYARD` records are not rejected for an inadmissible source.
- #strong[$I_5$ (primary-source and digitization gate).] Direct promotion ($upright("prom") = upright("true")$) is rejected if any $t in T^(+)$ has `requires_figure_digitization = True` or `primary_pdf_verified = False`.
- #strong[$I_6$ (anchor disjointness, claim attestations, and non-prose surface locks).] $T^(+) union T^(-) eq.not nothing$, $T^(+) inter T^(-) = nothing$, any populated `attestation_sha256` equals the freshly recomputed claim-attestation digest (`compute_claim_attestation_sha256`\; a mismatch raises `I6_STALE_CLAIM_ATTESTATION`), any optional `record_envelopes` sequence supplied to `verify_ceg_package` or `compute_ceg_section_digests` corresponds bijectively in order and canonical core-field values to the verified `records` (`verify_envelopes_bound_to_records`, raising `I6_ENVELOPE_RECORD_COUNT_MISMATCH` or `I6_ENVELOPE_RECORD_MISMATCH`), and any serialized claim envelope matches the expected non-prose surface digest passed to `verify_non_prose_surface_lock` across 100% of keys outside `ACTIVE_PROSE_KEYS` (`I6_NON_PROSE_METADATA_TAMPER`). The attestation comparison is evaluated without short-circuiting the semantic $I_1$–$I_5$ checks. Within `verify_ceg_package`, both digest comparisons (plus envelope `attestation_sha256` binding) belong to Layer 2 below; the anchor, disjointness, and non-digest envelope-to-record binding conditions are checked in both layers.

#strong[Two-layer package verification (`verify_ceg_package`).] In plain terms, Layer 1 checks the content itself and rejects structural or semantic tampering even if every stored checksum has been deleted, and Layer 2 adds checksum comparisons that catch changes the content checks cannot see, such as a reworded rationale. To ensure that cryptographic digests never substitute for structural checking, package verification separates into two explicit layers:

- #strong[Layer 1 (`check_top_digests=False`, structure-only closure):] Verifies graph integrity, every pointer and old-value guard of the ledger, exact equality of the forward- and reverse-replayed corpora with $D_m$ and $D_0$, bidirectional atom-to-corpus parity, and the non-digest conditions of $I_1$ and $I_3$–$I_6$ on every claim record, #strong[without] consulting any precomputed digest: the per-event study digests, the claim-attestation digests, and the section digests are all ignored. Any structural, catalogue, value, dangling-reference, or semantic tamper fails Layer 1 even if every stored digest is omitted or zeroed.
- #strong[Layer 2 (`check_top_digests=True`, digest locks):] Additionally checks the per-event study `pre`/`post` digests (completing $I_2$ as defined above), each record’s `attestation_sha256`, and, when section digests are supplied, the five locks computed by `compute_ceg_section_digests`: `initial_corpus_sha256` $= H\(D_0\)$, `settled_corpus_sha256` $= H\(D_m\)$, `ledger_sha256` $= H\(cal(L)\)$, `atoms_sha256`, and `records_non_prose_sha256`. Layer 2 catches what replay of values cannot see: reordered same-study events and stale attestations through the per-event and attestation digests, and rationale rewording, event renaming, inserted edit-and-revert pairs, and metadata injected outside `prose_sentences` through the section digests.

#figblock[#figimage("figures/fig2_two_regime_workflow.pdf")
#emph[Figure 2. Two-regime workflow and the `V3` commit verifier. Regime 1 (left) is the open-ended exploration and hypothesis-discovery loop over the in-context graph, the primary operating mode; it is a statement of design intent and is neither constrained nor evaluated in this paper. Candidate commits (`SynthesisClaimRecord`s with their `TypedProposition`s, and `ReversiblePatchEvent` curation edits) enter Regime 2, the two-layer verifier that acts as its guardrail: Layer 1 checks every structural, replay, lint, typed, and context invariant directly, and Layer 2 adds the per-step, attestation, and section digest locks. Accepted commits persist to the shared graph; rejected commits return structured violation codes to the context for iterative repair.]

]
=== 4.2 Hardened prose lint specification ($I_3^(upright("tok"))$ and $I_3^(upright("clause"))$)
<hardened-prose-lint-specification-i_3texttok-and-i_3textclause>
#emph[In plain terms:] the lint reads each evidence-anchor sentence as a strict proofreader would. It refuses invisible and look-alike characters (rule 1), reads units with their exact letter case (rule 2), splits the sentence into clauses and requires each clause’s numbers to come from the findings that clause cites (rule 3), flips negated bounds and requires an explicit comparison word when a clause names both arms of a multi-arm outcome (rule 4), and checks direction words such as #emph[reduced] or #emph[rose] clause by clause (rule 5). The lint is conservative by design: an ambiguous sentence is refused, which is why some true sentences fail (§6.4 and §6.8).

Because prose checks operate on surface strings, every rule and boundary is stated explicitly:

+ #strong[Unicode character and script security (`UTS #39` / `UTR #36`).] `contains_forbidden_unicode` rejects format (`Cf`), control (`Cc` outside `\t`, `\n`, `\r`), surrogate (`Cs`), private-use (`Co`), unassigned (`Cn`), line/paragraph separator (`Zl`, `Zp`), and #strong[all non-ASCII space (`Zs`)] characters (`U+00A0`, `U+2005`, `U+2009`), any default-ignorable code point, every #strong[enclosing mark (`Me`, such as `U+20DD`)], and any #strong[combining mark (`Mn`/`Mc`, such as `U+0301`) that adjoins an ASCII digit, ASCII letter, decimal separator, sign, whitespace, or a string boundary] (a combining mark between two non-ASCII letters is not flagged). Separately, `contains_confusable_homoglyphs` rejects Cyrillic, Armenian, and Cherokee letters, Greek letters confusable with Latin, mathematical-alphanumeric and fullwidth look-alikes, the Kelvin and Ångström signs, and mixed-script words, both in prose (`I3_TOK_CONFUSABLE_HOMOGLYPH`, for example `PR\u041e-C3` or `r\u043ese`) and in atom endpoint names, units, and arm identifiers at $I_1$. Scientific symbols remain permitted (`±`, `×`, `–`, `µ`/`μ`), and superscript or subscript digits are accepted inside registered endpoint and unit strings (for example an endpoint `VO₂max` or a unit `mL/min/1.73 m²`); elsewhere in prose they are refused as `I3_TOK_NONCANONICAL_NUMERAL`.
+ #strong[Case-sensitive SI units, `%` right boundaries, and designator guards.] `UNIT_TABLE` distinguishes case-sensitive SI concentration prefixes (`M`, `mM`, `uM`/`µM`, `nM`, `pM`, `fM`) from length prefixes (`m`, `cm`, `mm`, `um`/`µm`, `nm`, `pm`) without case-folding two-letter SI prefix symbols, attaches `%` cleanly before punctuation or parentheses (`94.2%.`, `94.2% (`) without trailing `\b` failure, checks both leading (`PRO-C3 (mg/mL) was 29.4`) and trailing (`concentration was 10 (um)`) parenthesized unit headers, and reads the single-letter units `d`, `h`, `l`, `m`, and `g` only when a space or hyphen separates them from their numeral (`28 d`, `28-d`\; a glued `28d` is refused as `I3_TOK_NONCANONICAL_NUMERAL`), suppressing `d` and `h` after a figure/table panel designator (`Fig. 28 d`, `Table 2 h`). Lowercase `l` and `ml` are read as litres and millilitres.
+ #strong[Depth-0 per-clause scoping and contrastive subordinators.] Multi-tuple sentences are split at bracket depth 0 across semicolons, coordinating/contrastive markers (`whereas`, `while`, `but`, and `and` or a clause comma before a citation), #strong[and mid-sentence contrastive subordinators with or without commas (`although`, `even though`, `though`)], while coordinating citation tails (`(C500:O2) and (C500:O1)`, `; see C500:O2 and C500:O1`) and leading `For C500:O1 and C500:O2,` headers are retained in pooled scope rather than mis-split into single-tuple fragments. Every clause carrying its own tuple citation(s) must ground its numeric/unit tokens in #emph[that clause]’s cited tuple pool—rejecting $k$-tuple cyclic permutations ($k gt.eq 3$), one-way cross-clause thefts, and duplicate-token thefts even when every value appears somewhere in the sentence union.
+ #strong[Symbolic/`never`-negated inequalities and conservative multi-arm binding.] Strict inequality extraction inverts symbolic and verbal negated bounds (`not < 5%` $arrow.r gt.eq 5 %$, `not <= 5%` $arrow.r > 5 %$, `never > 28` $arrow.r lt.eq 28$, `never less than 5%` $arrow.r gt.eq 5 %$), and conservatively refuses residual bare numeric negation (`not 24 h`) or stacked double negation (`not never < 28 days`): the operator parser returns `AMBIGUOUS_NEGATION`, which matches no atom relation, so the clause fails with `I3_CLAUSE_INEQUALITY_MISMATCH`. On pairwise outcomes, clauses naming an unmodeled study arm alongside a bound arm fail with `I3_CLAUSE_MULTI_ARM_AMBIGUITY`\; across multi-arm outcomes, clauses naming both focal and comparator arms without an explicit contrast connector (`vs`, `versus`, `compared with`, `compared to`, `relative to`, `against`, `than`, `in contrast to`, `as opposed to`, `whereas`, `while`, `but`, `even though`, `although`, `though`, `respectively`, `over` introducing an arm reference, or a `from`–`to` trajectory over the two point values) fail with `I3_CLAUSE_NON_CONTRAST_MULTI_ARM` (refusing symmetric #emph[“Arm A and Arm B”] and parenthetical side-notes), and comma-appositive comparative arm swaps (#emph[“the placebo arm had a change, $- 20.5 med upright("mL")$, compared with treatment”]) are rejected with `I3_CLAUSE_ARM_VALUE_SWAP`.
+ #strong[Clause-local polarity windowing.] Comparator-arm governance resets across `;` or `:` and across `and`, `but`, or `yet` followed directly by a single-token subject before the cue (`_CLAUSE_RESET_RE`), so an absent-reduction clause on a comparator (#emph[“the reduction seen in vehicle slices was absent and PRO-C3 rose to … in treated slices”]) cannot mask an inverted verb on the focal arm (`I3_CLAUSE_DIRECTION_REVERSAL`), trailing polarity clauses after `respectively` lists are checked against all cited atoms, and cues asserted of the comparator arm while denied of the focal arm (#emph[“seen in vehicle slices, not in treated slices”]) fail with `I3_CLAUSE_NEGATED_DIRECTION`.

=== 4.3 Formal results
<formal-results>
#emph[In plain terms:] if the edit log checks out, replaying it forward from the raw extraction gives exactly the curated data and replaying it backward gives exactly the raw extraction; and the checksum-guarded replay fails whenever either endpoint corpus is altered while the other and the edit log are fixed, an edit is dropped or repeated, two adjacent edits to the same study are swapped, or an edit is pointed somewhere else.

#strong[Proposition 1 (state-guarded bijective patch replay).] #emph[Assume SHA-256 is collision resistant on `RFC 8785` canonical encodings of float-free JSON documents. If $\(D_0\,D_m\,cal(L)\)$ satisfies $I_2$, then:] #emph[\(i) (determinism) forward replay of $cal(L)$ from $D_0$ yields exactly $D_m$\;] #emph[\(ii) (invertibility) reverse replay of $\(e_m^(- 1)\,dots.h\,e_1^(- 1)\)$ from $D_m$ yields exactly $D_0$\; and] #emph[\(iii) (tamper evidence) digest-guarded replay—`verify_bijective_patch_replay` with its per-step study digests, which is $I_2$ as defined in §4.1 and what Layer 2 runs—fails for (a) any altered $D_0$ with $\(D_m\,cal(L)\)$ fixed or any altered $D_m$ with $\(D_0\,cal(L)\)$ fixed, (b) deletion or duplication of any event $e_k$, (c) transposition of adjacent events touching the same study, (d) replacement of $upright("ptr")_k$ by any distinct pointer (including non-canonical index aliases), and (e) any pointer escaping the subtree of study $s_k$.]

#emph[Proof.] Canonical `RFC 8785` serialization is injective on float-free JSON trees and deep-copies study subtrees so no mutable container is shared across studies. At step $i$, forward replay verifies $H\(W_(i - 1)\[s_i\]\)= H_i^(upright("pre"))$ and $W_(i - 1)\[upright("ptr")_i\]= v_i^(upright("old"))$, writes $v_i^(upright("new"))$ at $upright("ptr")_i$ to form $W_i$, and verifies $H\(W_i\[s_i\]\)= H_i^(upright("post"))$\; terminal equality $H\(W_m\)= H\(D_m\)$ yields $W_m = D_m$ under collision resistance, proving (i). Because $upright("ptr")_i$ addresses an existing location holding $v_i^(upright("old"))$, writing $v_i^(upright("old"))$ back into $W_i$ restores $W_(i - 1)$ and satisfies the swapped guards $\(H_i^(upright("post"))\,H_i^(upright("pre"))\)$, proving (ii) by induction from $m$ down to $0$. For (iii): (a) with $\(D_m\,cal(L)\)$ fixed, any $D'_0 eq.not D_0$ satisfying all step guards would map by (ii) from its terminal state $W'_m$ back to $D'_0 eq.not D_0$, forcing $W'_m eq.not D_m$ and breaking terminal equality, and symmetrically for $D'_m eq.not D_m$ with $\(D_0\,cal(L)\)$ fixed; (b) since $v_k^(upright("old")) eq.not v_k^(upright("new"))$, omitting or repeating $e_k$ alters $W_k\[s_k\]$ and breaks either the next guard on $s_k$ or terminal equality with $D_m$\; (c) for adjacent events on $s_k$, $H_k^(upright("pre")) eq.not H_k^(upright("post"))$ because $v_k^(upright("old")) eq.not v_k^(upright("new"))$, so $e_(k + 1)$ encounters $H_k^(upright("pre")) eq.not H_(k + 1)^(upright("pre"))$\; (d) non-canonical pointer tokens fail Definition 5(a) directly, while any distinct canonical pointer either fails the old-value guard, leaves two differing locations in $W_k\[s_k\]$ (disjoint paths), or would require a finite JSON subtree to equal a proper sub-value of itself (nested paths), breaking $H_k^(upright("post"))$\; and (e) subtree containment is checked directly on $upright("ptr")_k$. $square.stroked.tiny$

#emph[Remark (commuting cross-study edits and Layer-2 digest binding).] Adjacent events on #emph[distinct] studies commute and represent the same per-study state transition; replay accepts their transposition unless event digests are hash-chained. Likewise, rewording `rationale`, renaming `event_id`, or inserting an edit followed immediately by its exact inverse preserves the map $D_0 mapsto D_m$ and is accepted by replay, while altering $H\(cal(L)\)$ and failing Layer-2 digest verification (Table 8). Because per-event digests cover only studies touched by $cal(L)$, an identical modification applied simultaneously to an unpatched study in both $D_0$ and $D_m$ also preserves the relative transition $D_0 mapsto D_m$ under standalone replay and is caught in Layer 2 by the whole-corpus endpoint locks (`initial_corpus_sha256`, `settled_corpus_sha256`). Layer 1 verifies pointers, old-value guards, and exact equality of the replayed corpora but does not consult the per-step digests, so part (iii)(c) is not guaranteed there: adjacent events on the #emph[same] study that edit disjoint locations also commute under value-only replay, and their transposition is accepted by Layer 1 and rejected by Layer 2.

#emph[In plain terms:] (1) provided a result that is valid in one setting stays valid in every setting that it covers, whenever the gate allows a number to be carried directly from one setting to another the transfer is sound, and when the gate does not allow it, it names exactly the axes that fall short; (2) as soon as two settings cannot be ranked against each other, such as a mouse model and human tissue slices, no single evidence score with a cut-off can allow all of the sound transfers and only those.

#strong[Theorem 1 (poset transfer soundness and impossibility of a faithful scalar evidence ranking).] #emph[\(1) Suppose validity of a quantitative parameter within an endpoint family is monotone down $\(cal(C)\,scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C))\)$: whenever it holds at $c in cal(C)$, it holds at every $c' scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c$. If $I_4$ permits direct quantitative promotion from $c_s$ to $c_t$, then validity at $c_s$ implies validity at $c_t$\; otherwise $U\(c_s\,c_t\)$ is the unique minimal set of axes on which $c_s$ must be upgraded to cover $c_t$.] #emph[\(2) Suppose $cal(C)$ contains at least one incomparable pair $c^(\(1\)) scripts(parallel)_(cal(C)) c^(\(2\))$. For any scalar ranking $r : cal(C) arrow.r bb(R)$ and threshold $theta in bb(R)$, define $T_(r\,theta)\(c\,c'\)arrow.l.r.double r\(c\)- r\(c'\)gt.eq theta$. No rule $T_(r\,theta)$ is simultaneously complete ($forall c' scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c\,med T_(r\,theta)\(c\,c'\)$) and sound ($forall c\,c'\,med T_(r\,theta)\(c\,c'\)arrow.r.double.long c' scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c$).]

#emph[Proof.] (1) $I_4$ permits direct promotion iff $tau_b\(c_s\,c_t\)= tt("DIRECT_MATCH") arrow.l.r.double U\(c_s\,c_t\)= nothing arrow.l.r.double forall k in D\,med c_(t\,k) scripts(prec.curly.eq)_k c_(s\,k) arrow.l.r.double c_t scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c_s$, whence monotonicity yields validity at $c_t$. If $k in U\(c_s\,c_t\)$, any context $c'$ with $c'_k = c_(s\,k)$ has $c_(t\,k) scripts(prec.curly.eq.not)_k c'_k$ and fails to cover $c_t$, whereas setting $c'_k = c_(t\,k)$ for $k in U\(c_s\,c_t\)$ and $c'_k = c_(s\,k)$ otherwise satisfies $c_t scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c'$. (2) Reflexivity $c^(\(1\)) scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c^(\(1\))$ and completeness give $r\(c^(\(1\))\)- r\(c^(\(1\))\)= 0 gt.eq theta$. Without loss of generality $r\(c^(\(1\))\)gt.eq r\(c^(\(2\))\)$, so $r\(c^(\(1\))\)- r\(c^(\(2\))\)gt.eq 0 gt.eq theta$, licensing transfer from $c^(\(1\))$ to $c^(\(2\))$ despite $c^(\(2\)) scripts(prec.curly.eq.not)_(cal(C)) c^(\(1\))$ \[28, 29\]. $square.stroked.tiny$

#emph[In plain terms:] when one sentence cites two outcomes of the same study that share arms, units, and direction, a checker that looks only at the citations, numbers, units, and arm and direction words cannot tell the true sentence from the one that attaches the right numbers to the wrong outcome, so it must get half of such sentences wrong.

#strong[Theorem 2 (surface-verifier indistinguishability under pooled citations).] #emph[Let $Phi\(upright("cl")\)=\(T_(upright("cl"))\,M_(upright("cl"))\,pi_(upright("cl"))\)$ be the projection of a clause $upright("cl")$ onto its resolved citation scope $T_(upright("cl"))$, its sequence of extracted numeral–unit mentions $M_(upright("cl"))$, and its sequence of arm and polarity cues $pi_(upright("cl"))$. Call any checker whose verdict on $upright("cl")$ is a function solely of $Phi\(upright("cl")\)$ and ${ cal(A)\[t\]: t in T_(upright("cl")) }$ a] surface verifier. #emph[Let $o_1 eq.not o_2$ be two outcomes of one study sharing identical arms, units, and polarity, distinct numeric values, and endpoint names contributing identical numerals and cues to $Phi$. Let $s_(i j)$ denote the sentence naming endpoint $o_i$, quoting $o_j$’s values in focal-then-comparator order, and citing $\(o_1\,o_2\)$ in a pooled scope $T = { o_1\,o_2 }$. Then $Phi\(s_12\)= Phi\(s_22\)$ and $Phi\(s_21\)= Phi\(s_11\)$, so every surface verifier errs on exactly two of the four sentences ${ s_11\,s_22\,s_12\,s_21 }$.]

#emph[Proof.] Because $o_1$ and $o_2$ share arms, units, and direction, $s_11$ and $s_22$ are true while $s_12$ and $s_21$ misbind $o_j$’s values to $o_i$’s endpoint. Sentences $s_12$ and $s_22$ have identical citation scope ${ o_1\,o_2 }$, identical numeral–unit sequence $M\(o_2\)$, and—because their endpoint noun phrases contribute identical numerals and cues—identical $pi_(upright("cl"))$. Thus $Phi\(s_12\)= Phi\(s_22\)$ and symmetrically $Phi\(s_21\)= Phi\(s_11\)$, forcing any function of $\(Phi\,{ cal(A)\[o_1\]\,cal(A)\[o_2\]}\)$ to return one verdict on ${ s_12\,s_22 }$ and one verdict on ${ s_21\,s_11 }$, making exactly one error per pair. $square.stroked.tiny$

#emph[In plain terms:] on the indistinguishable pooled pairs of Theorem 2, refusing the pooled clause is necessary to block the misbound sentences, so the reference implementation adopts blanket refusal of any numeral-bearing pooled clause as a conservative sufficient policy (which also refuses some true pooled sentences); writing one sentence per outcome avoids the problem, and the reference implementation generates an accepted sentence of that kind for every finding in the benchmark graphs.

#strong[Corollary 2.1 (sound pooled policy and single-outcome canonical rendering).] #emph[Within the class of surface verifiers, on every indistinguishability class of Theorem 2, rejecting the pooled sentences is necessary to reject all pooled misbindings $s_12\,s_21$, at the cost of refusing the true pooled sentences $s_11\,s_22$\; rejecting every numeral-bearing clause whose scope has $\|T_(upright("cl"))\|gt.eq 2$ (`pooled_numeral_policy="reject"`) is therefore adopted as a conservative sufficient policy across all pooled clauses. Splitting an $m$-outcome pooled claim into $m$ single-outcome clauses avoids pooled scope ($\|T_(upright("cl"))\|= 1$ on each clause), and `render_atom_sentence` provides a deterministic single-outcome rendering accepted by the strict lint across all 242/242 benchmark and hostile-name atoms (Table 8).]

`render_atom_sentence` relies on lexicon masking rather than an unconditional guarantee over arbitrary strings. The verbatim endpoint phrase and any numeral-bearing unit string are registered in the graph’s masking lexicon, so ordinary digits, superscripts, Roman numerals, and direction words inside them are not read as standalone numerals or cues, and the bracket-balance check of $I_1$ keeps the citation scope resolvable (verified across 242/242 atoms in Table 8). Completability is non-universal when $I_1$-valid fields collide with prose tokenization or omit rendered operators: `render_atom_sentence` is refused if an outcome local identifier lacks the `O\d+` prose prefix required by `TUPLE_RE`, if a `relative_change` atom carries an inequality relation (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, which `render_atom_sentence` omits from its template), if an endpoint or unit string itself contains an outcome-tuple identifier, if a unit string contains an unmasked number word (`half`), direction cue (`lower`), or relation operator, or if a short registered endpoint in the graph (such as `>` or `5`) masks an adjacent relation symbol or decimal fraction in the rendered clause. Table 8 verifies single-outcome completability exhaustively on the benchmark graphs rather than claiming it for all strings. Theorem 2 characterizes pooled scopes only; a #emph[single-tuple] clause that cites `P900:O1`, states `P900:O1`’s true numbers, and writes the wrong endpoint noun phrase (`R1` single-tuple templates) remains outside surface checking and motivates grounding decisions on $I_3^(upright("typed"))$.

#emph[In plain terms:] a structured claim passes only if it matches its finding exactly on seven fields (the endpoint name, the two sets of arms, the two quantities, the statistic, and the sample size), so each finding has exactly one valid structured claim, apart from its identifier, the order in which arms are listed, and letter case or surrounding spaces in the endpoint name, and changing any single field is rejected.

#strong[Theorem 3 (unique seven-coordinate exactness of the typed layer).] #emph[For $a = cal(A)\[t\]$, define]

$ Pi\(a\)= #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\($] upright("norm")\(a.#h(0pt)upright("ep")\)\,med upright("set")\(a . gamma . A_(upright("focal"))\)\,med upright("set")\(a . gamma . A_(upright("comp"))\)\,med a . q_(upright("focal"))\,med a . q_(upright("comp"))\,med a.#h(0pt)upright("stat")\,med a . n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[$\)$]\, $

#emph[where $upright("norm")$ (`normalize_endpoint_name`) trims leading and trailing ASCII spaces and tabs and folds ASCII letters `A–Z` only; internal whitespace is compared as written. A duplicate-free `TypedProposition` $p$ referencing $t$ satisfies $I_3^(upright("typed"))$ iff its seven coordinates equal $Pi\(a\)$. Consequently: (i) each atom admits a unique valid typed proposition up to proposition ID, arm-tuple permutation, and the ASCII letter case and surrounding ASCII space of $upright("ep")$\; (ii) every single-coordinate mutation—including pooled endpoint–value swaps, focal/comparator arm-role swaps, comparator-quantity substitution, relative-vs-level scale conflation, statistic changes, and sample-size omissions or float coercions—is rejected; (iii) when $upright("prom") = upright("true")$, every $t in T^(+)$ is covered by such a proposition; and (iv) verification runs in $O\(\|A_(upright("focal"))\|log\|A_(upright("focal"))\|+\|A_(upright("comp"))\|log\|A_(upright("comp"))\|+\|upright("ep")\|+ L_(upright("lit"))\)$ time per proposition, where $L_(upright("lit"))$ is the total character length of the serialized arm identifiers and quantity literals.]

