== 6. Empirical Evaluation
<empirical-evaluation>
#strong[Scope of the evaluation.] Every experiment in this section exercises the deterministic commit gate (Regime 2 of §1): candidate claim records, typed propositions, evidence-anchor sentences, and curation ledgers are submitted to the verifier and its verdicts are counted. No experiment measures upstream exploration (Regime 1), and none estimates how often exploration yields a correct or novel hypothesis. Section 6.9 is a case study on a production corpus: it reports size, structure, and the replay of a correction log, and it does not evaluate hypothesis quality either.

=== 6.1 Benchmark architecture, ablation regimes, and dual-mode protocol
<benchmark-architecture-ablation-regimes-and-dual-mode-protocol>
The synthetic benchmark (`ceg_synthetic_benchmark.py`) comprises 1,931 cases—1,521 adversarial and 410 valid—instantiated from 199 distinct templates across five suites:

+ #strong[Core suite] (800 cases, 37 templates): ten adversarial strata `F1`–`F10` ($60$ each) and four valid strata `P1`–`P4` ($50$ each).
+ #strong[Stress suite] (800 cases, 59 templates): ten harder adversarial strata `F11`–`F20` ($60$ each) and four valid strata `P5`–`P8` ($50$ each), where `P5` (pooled citations) and `P7` (derived differences, unsigned magnitudes of negative changes, dispersion `±` terms, unmodeled trial-phase numerals, and cross-noun negation scope) measure the conservative lint’s refusal boundary.
+ #strong[Residual suite] (240 cases, 12 templates): four open-class adversarial prose strata `R1`–`R4` ($60$ each) designed to document surface-lint blind spots (misnamed endpoint nouns with true numbers, unitless sample-size swaps, qualitative overstatements, and unlisted implicit negations).
+ #strong[Red-team suite] (43 sentences, 43 templates): an independent adversarial pass, namely variations across `T5` and `T11`–`T18` authored by an independent reviewer against frozen release `v2.0`.
+ #strong[Gauntlet suite] (48 cases, 48 templates: 38 adversarial across `G1`–`G5` and 10 valid controls in `G0`): a domain-agnostic boundary stress suite, with probes targeting 3-tuple cyclic permutations, one-way/duplicate cross-clause value thefts, `although`/`though` subordinators, comma-appositive swaps, Unicode `Mn`/`Me`/`Zs` and `UTS #39` homoglyphs, SI prefix case collisions (`uM` vs `um`), panel designator collisions (`Fig. 28 d`), symbolic/`never`-negated inequalities, conservative multi-arm binding, and colon-bearing `PMID:`/`DOI:` identifiers.

#strong[Five nested ablation regimes] evaluate every case: `V0` (citation resolution only), `V1` (`V0` + study-wide ASCII numeral containment), `V2` (prose lint $I_1 + I_3^(upright("tok")) + I_3^(upright("clause"))$ with `pooled_numeral_policy="permit"`), `V2s` (prose lint with `pooled_numeral_policy="reject"`), and `V3` (full CEG verifier: $I_1$–$I_6$, strict lint, $I_3^(upright("typed"))$, $I_4$, $I_5$, and $I_6$). In plain terms, the regimes are five progressively stricter checkers, from one that asks only whether the cited finding exists (`V0`) to the full verifier (`V3`), so comparing them shows what each added layer catches. In the tables that follow, a higher count is better in every column: adversarial cases should be rejected, and valid cases should be accepted. A valid case that is refused is a cost of conservative linting and is not an error that reaches the graph.

#strong[Dual-mode (“Mode B self-attesting adversary”) protocol.] In plain terms, Mode A tests a naive edit, which a stale checksum alone would catch, whereas Mode B tests an edit that recomputes fresh checksums to disguise itself, so a Mode B rejection shows that the verifier caught the underlying scientific error rather than relying on a hash. When synthesis records carry precomputed attestation digests (`attestation_sha256`) and metadata hashes, mutating prose or fields without recomputing attestations (#strong[Mode A: raw tamper]) could appear to reject an attack merely because the attestation hash goes stale. The benchmark therefore evaluates all 1,161 record-level adversarial cases (all synthetic adversarial cases whose defect resides inside the claim record rather than corpus/graph corruption) under #strong[both Mode A and Mode B], where Mode B re-runs `populate_claim_attestation` after tampering and asserts that every rejection carries a genuine semantic invariant code ($I_1$–$I_6$ disjointness) rather than a stale-attestation error.

#strong[Development disclosure.] Earlier iterations of the reference implementation serve as pre-hardening baselines from benchmark development. The first release scored 600/600 and 200/200 on the core suite written alongside it, but rejected only 40/600 stress adversarial cases and accepted 107/200 stress valid cases. Frozen release `v2.0` closed most stress families but rejected only 1/43 on the independent red-team pass; frozen release `v2.1` rejected 580/600 on stress adversarial, 36/43 on red-team, and 20/38 on the new gauntlet adversarial suite (while falsely rejecting 2 of 10 gauntlet valid controls: `8/10` accepted). All synthetic suites are therefore in-sample regression benchmarks for `v3.1`, whereas §6.8 evaluates primary-source clinical trial abstracts from PubMed.

