Across all 1,161 record-level adversarial cases, Mode B self-attesting tampering achieves 1,161/1,161 semantic rejections (`0` escapes), so in this benchmark an attestation hash never masks a semantic weakness, while `verify_non_prose_surface_lock` rejects 60/60 metadata injections and edits outside `prose_sentences`. Two execution-hygiene points matter for anyone building a synthesis pipeline on this code. First, Python can load cached bytecode (a `.pyc` file) in place of the source file, so a tampered cache could silently replace the verifier. `load_source_pinned_module` avoids this by checking the SHA-256 of the `.py` source bytes and compiling those bytes directly with `__cached__ = None`, which defeats even a poisoned `PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX` bytecode file crafted to match the source file’s exact modification timestamp and byte length when `-B` is omitted. Second, zero `assert` statements appear anywhere in the live codebase, so `python3 -O`, which strips assertions, executes 100% of checks identically.

=== 6.8 Primary-source real-text evaluation on peer-reviewed PubMed studies
<primary-source-real-text-evaluation-on-peer-reviewed-pubmed-studies>
To measure how CEG verification behaves beyond author-constructed synthetic templates, `ceg_real_text_evaluation.py` evaluates 12 peer-reviewed PubMed studies—four in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (`INPULSIS-1/2` \[33\], `ASCEND` \[34\], `BI 1015550` Phase 2 \[35\], and human IPF fibroblast `MRTF` inhibition \[36\]), four in heart failure (`PARADIGM-HF` \[37\], `PARAGON-HF` \[38\], `VICTORIA` \[39\], and post-MI rat `LCZ696` remodeling \[40\]), and four in thoracic oncology (`KEYNOTE-024` \[41\], `AURA3` \[42\], `ADAURA` \[43\], and preclinical `EGFR` exon 20 insertion models \[44\]). Together these papers yield 31 `EvidenceAtom`s spanning `point_level`, `relative_change`, `interval_bound`, and `censored_observation` quantities over a five-axis biomedical context lattice (`evidence_tier`, `indication`, `population_stratum`, `followup_horizon`, `readout_level`) built by `build_clinical_translational_lattice`. Raw corpus $D_0$ (SHA-256 prefix `bad3c6d29dab`) undergoes 4 reversible `JSONPatch` curation events—one repairing an OCR `um` $arrow.r$ `uM` SI case fold on `PMID:36585256`, two restoring an arm-swapped `PARADIGM-HF` primary event rate (the focal and the comparator value), and one correcting an ITT-versus-CNS subgroup sample size ($419 arrow.r 144$) on `AURA3`—to reach settled corpus $D_4$ (SHA-256 prefix `adda477aab37`), passing bidirectional $I_1$ binding and $I_2$ replay with zero errors.

The evaluation has two tracks. Track A tests raw sentences copied verbatim from published PubMed abstracts. Track B tests structured multi-study syntheses and realistic clinical translation mistakes built over the same studies.

#strong[Track A: Verbatim PubMed abstract sentences ($n = 36$, 3 per study).] Track A tests verbatim sentences extracted directly from the published PubMed XML abstracts both unanchored (raw journal sentences with no CEG tuple tag) and source-anchored (prepending the true study outcome tag(s) `R101:O1`, etc., with verbatim body text untouched).

