=== 6.9 Production-scale case study: a type 1 diabetes corpus of 1,151 studies
<production-scale-case-study-a-type-1-diabetes-corpus-of-1151-studies>
#emph[In plain terms:] the earlier sections test the checks on invented cases and on 12 curated studies. This section looks at what happens when the same representation is applied to a real corpus with roughly a hundred times as many studies, built for an engineering project on type 1 diabetes. It reports the corpus’s size and structure, replays its correction log through the reference verifier, and says plainly what the corpus, as it stood in a dated snapshot, does not show. It is a case study and not a benchmark: no hypothesis is scored.

#strong[The corpus.] The corpus comes from the OpenIslet project, which compiles studies on islet replacement and on immune-evasive stem-cell-derived islets (OpenIslet, openislet.org, 12 November 2026 snapshot). Every figure and status statement in this section describes that snapshot, and later states of the corpus may differ. The snapshot is not bundled inside this package and is identified by SHA-256 digests (Code and Data Availability). Of 3,922 screened records (3,921 distinct identifier pairs), 3,246 advanced at title-and-abstract screening, and 1,151 studies were extracted into structured records: 747 of the candidate records and all 404 advanced records of the project’s earlier core list. The other 2,095 advanced records, a lower-priority supporting catalog, were not extracted, so the corpus is a prioritized subset of the advanced literature and not a census of it. Screening, extraction, and verification were carried out by language models working as extractor, verifier, and adjudicator, with deterministic validation of each extraction round, as that project’s runbook records; the models are named in the AI Contribution Statement. OpenIslet is an open-source project led by the author of this paper (Funding and Competing Interests). Ten findings from eight of its studies are the worked example of §5, where each is classified against a clinical target context and submitted to the gate. Table 10 summarizes the result.

