    [Size], [settled corpus 20,412,758 bytes in canonical JSON (17.7 kB per study; 1.4 kB per outcome) and 25,520,705 bytes as stored; 41,746,322 bytes of raw extraction packets; three single-window Markdown views of 9,644,648, 3,677,507, and 3,239,524 bytes (16,561,679 in total); full indexed graph 70,625,398 bytes],
    table.hline(stroke: 0.9pt),
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Size.] The figures in the last row of Table 10 are byte counts and not token counts. In the canonical JSON form used in §7.3, the settled corpus occupies about 6.0 times as much per study as the 12-study corpus of §6.8. The difference lies in how many outcomes were extracted from each study and not in the size of a record: about 12.3 outcomes per study against about 2.6 outcomes per study, at 1.4 kB per outcome (arm and link records included) against 1.1 kB per outcome. The project also renders three Markdown views of the corpus, each intended to be read in a single context window, of 3.2 to 9.6 million bytes. At a rule of thumb of 3 to 4 bytes per token, which was not measured here, the smallest view is of the order of 0.8 to 1.1 million tokens, the largest of 2.4 to 3.2 million tokens, and the canonical settled corpus of 5.1 to 6.8 million tokens. Whether a view fits therefore depends on the context window of the model, and the complete corpus is several times larger than its smallest view. No model was run on any of these views in this paper.

#strong[Correction log at scale.] The corpus’s curation ledger holds 106 reversible events. Each is one edit to one study’s record, with the old value, the new value, a reason, and digests of that study’s record before and after: 25 events from the first to the second corpus version, 16 from the second to the third, and 65 from the third to the fourth. They edit outcome records (89 events), study-to-design links (9), arm lists (5), and study-design fields (3). Replaying the ledger forward from the first pinned corpus version and backward from the fourth reproduces the pinned payloads exactly, with no old-value guard or study-digest failure in either direction under the project’s own digest convention. The reference implementation’s replay verifier (invariant $I_2$, Proposition 1) then accepts the same ledger over the 1,151-study corpus in Layer 1 and in Layer 2, once the ledger is adapted to the corpus shape that the verifier expects and its study digests are recomputed in the canonical form of `RFC 8785` (106 events, 29 patched studies). Because those digests are recomputed, Layer 2 acceptance of the unaltered ledger is expected by construction; the informative results are the exact replay in both directions, the check of the ledger’s own stored digests, and the tamper controls. Eight kinds of tamper, run as ten controls, behave at this scale as Proposition 1 predicts. Layer 1 rejects 5 of 5 structural tampers without consulting any digest: deleting an event, duplicating one, altering one string in either corpus, and redirecting a pointer. Transposing two adjacent events of one study that edit different fields, and overwriting one per-step digest, are accepted by Layer 1 and rejected by Layer 2. Rewording one rationale is accepted by replay but changes the ledger digest.

#strong[Dossiers and the project’s own validator.] The project compiled the corpus into bulk dossiers of cross-study failure modes, scoping qualifications, and upgrade hypotheses, written through rounds of language-model review. Their active records cite 1,565 outcome tuples across 320 studies, and 407 indexed dossier records (378 distinct identifiers) were checked by the project’s own validator, which according to the snapshot’s own record visited every active record (11,116 numeric-token occurrences checked, none unvisited). That validator is a separate implementation of the same design and is not part of this package. These counts are therefore reported for scale and are not results of the reference implementation. Of the 1,565 cited outcome tuples, 139 carry a point-level quantity and 1,426 are typed as missing numbers, so the exact typed layer (Theorem 3) can apply to only a small fraction of the corpus in this snapshot. The dossiers’ own verdicts authorize no numeric promotion: every upgrade candidate is held pending prospective falsification. That is the behavior the commit gate is designed to produce when no outcome has been certified against its primary source (invariant $I_5$).

#strong[What the case study shows and does not show.] It shows that the representation and the verification of a correction log operate on a corpus of 1,151 studies and 14,105 outcomes, and it makes the limits of that corpus visible. It does not show that the extraction is accurate, because in this snapshot none of the outcomes has been certified against its primary source and 21.4% are pinned to an abstract rather than to full-text results. It does not show coverage, because 2,095 advanced records were not extracted. It does not show that a model can use such a corpus in context, because no model was run on it and the complete corpus is several times larger than its smallest single-window view. It does not show the quality of the hypotheses in the dossiers, because none is scored. Section 7.3 states these limits again among the limits of the whole evaluation.

== 7. Discussion
<discussion>
=== 7.1 Exact guarantees versus heuristic lint boundaries
<exact-guarantees-versus-heuristic-lint-boundaries>
#emph[In plain terms:] four parts of the design are exact (the structured claims, the edit log, the rule for carrying results between settings, and the metadata locks), and six things lie outside what the checks can guarantee. Both lists matter to anyone relying on a CEG.

Four properties are exact relative to the declared inputs: (1) a `TypedProposition` passing $I_3^(upright("typed"))$ equals its target `EvidenceAtom` on all seven coordinates, and every promoted claim covers all supporting tuples (Theorem 3); (2) a corpus pair and ledger passing $I_2$ determine one another bijectively, and digest-guarded replay rejects all single-event deletions, same-study transpositions, pointer aliases, and cross-study pointer escapes, with replay-neutral metadata edits pinned by $H\(cal(L)\)$ in Layer 2 (Proposition 1); (3) direct quantitative promotion under $I_4$ licenses only monotone transfers $c_t scripts(prec.curly.eq)_(cal(C)) c_s$ on the declared product poset $Lambda$, avoiding the inevitable false transfers or false exclusions of scalar rankings on incomparable models (Theorem 1); and (4) non-prose metadata surface locks ($I_6$) prevent unverified strings from bypassing verification outside `prose_sentences`, verified under a self-attesting Mode B adversary.

Six boundaries remain outside formal guarantees and must be stated plainly:

+ #strong[Upstream extraction fidelity.] No invariant checks $D_0$ against the source PDF. If an extractor misreads a table cell into $D_0$ and no curation patch corrects it, $I_1$–$I_6$ certify coherence with the erroneous atom. The production corpus snapshot of §6.9 makes this limit concrete: its outcomes were extracted by language models, 21.4% of them are pinned to an abstract, and at the time of the snapshot none had been certified against its primary source.
+ #strong[Specification of the context poset $Lambda$.] Theorem 1 guarantees soundness #emph[relative to $Lambda$]. Omitting a relevant validity axis (such as dose level in the synthetic bundle of §5.4) collapses distinct clinical cohorts onto one context element; $Lambda$ is a scientific modeling commitment that must be peer-reviewed alongside the graph.
+ #strong[Open-class free prose.] As Theorem 2 and `R1`–`R4` (`220/240` undetected) demonstrate, surface prose linting cannot resolve arbitrary endpoint noun phrases, unitless role swaps, or open-class rhetorical negations. Decision-making consumers must read $cal(P)_(upright("typed"))$, treating $Sigma_(upright("prose"))$ as human-facing commentary constrained by a conservative lint.
+ #strong[Derived cross-atom quantities and rich clinical syntax.] Track A (`30/36` refusals on verbatim PubMed sentences) and `P7` (`0/50` accepted) quantify the rewrite cost: inline confidence intervals, hazard ratios paired with event rates, numerator/denominator counts, and cross-arm differences must currently either be decomposed into canonical tuple-scoped clauses or await typed derived-arithmetic extensions.
+ #strong[Prospective falsifier gates.] Requiring a `ProspectiveFalsifierGate` on a `BOUNDED_BRIDGE` records the exact unresolved axes and a quantitative pass threshold; it does not empirically validate the bridge prior to running the experiment \[31, 32\].
+ #strong[Screening coverage of structured strings.] Forbidden-Unicode screening covers prose sentences; atom identifiers, endpoint names, units, arm identifiers, interval strings, and provenance fields; falsifier-gate fields; record and proposition identifiers; and ledger event identifiers, study identifiers, pointers, and rationales. Homoglyph screening covers prose sentences, endpoint names, units, and arm identifiers. Strings outside these lists, such as arm aliases and auxiliary envelope metadata, are bound by exact matching and by the Layer-2 digests rather than inspected. Single-outcome canonical rendering (`render_atom_sentence`) likewise depends on the prose-compatibility conditions documented in §4.3.

=== 7.2 Practical deployment in LLM drafting loops: explore free in context, verify at commit
<practical-deployment-in-llm-drafting-loops-explore-free-in-context-verify-at-commit>
The primary use of a CEG is to give researchers and language models a way to load a large structured literature into context, explore it freely, and generate hypotheses, and the experimental bridges that would test them, that stay tied to the recorded findings. Deterministic verification serves that use: it keeps the shared evidence graph clean as it grows, and it does not restrict exploration. The intended deployment follows the two regimes of §1 and Figure 2. In Regime 1 the model works over the in-context graph of `EvidenceAtom`s and `ContextSignature` coordinates without constraint; nothing in the CEG design lints or restricts its exploratory reasoning, intermediate drafts, or narrative discussion. In Regime 2 only candidate commits reach the verifier. Full `V3` verification is deterministic, uses only the Python standard library, and calls no model, so a drafting agent can submit a candidate `SynthesisClaimRecord`, receive structured violation codes (`I3_TOK_UNGROUNDED_VALUE`, `I3_CLAUSE_AMBIGUOUS_POOLED_CITATION`, `I4_MISSING_PROSPECTIVE_FALSIFIER_GATE`, `I4_INCOMPLETE_PROSPECTIVE_FALSIFIER_AXES`), and repair the draft iteratively. No latency measurement is reported for this loop.

The commit gate is strict about context transfer rather than advisory. A `SUPPORTS` or `CANDIDATE_UPGRADE` record is rejected (`I4_INADMISSIBLE_CONTEXT_TRANSFER`) when any of its supporting tuples is an `INADMISSIBLE_TRANSFER`\; a `CANDIDATE_UPGRADE` over a `BOUNDED_BRIDGE` passes only with a `ProspectiveFalsifierGate` covering every unresolved axis and is then held (`HOLD_FOR_PROSPECTIVE_FALSIFIER`) rather than promoted; and direct promotion requires `DIRECT_MATCH` on every supporting tuple. A cross-context observation that does not meet these conditions can still be recorded as a `SCOPING_QUALIFICATION`, which is how moderator explanations enter the graph.

The rewrite cost measured on Track A (`30/36` verbatim abstract sentences refused, §6.8) applies only to the evidence-anchor sentences that enter the commit gate as `prose_sentences`. It is not a cost on the model’s free-form exploration, reasoning, or narrative discussion, which the verifier never sees. These statements describe design intent: the benchmarks of §6 evaluate the commit gate only, and no experiment in this paper measures exploration quality or discovery rates.

Two deployment safeguards follow directly from these findings: (i) reward signals or acceptance gates must require `TypedProposition` coverage rather than prose-lint passage alone, preventing models from drifting into `R1`–`R4` paraphrases; and (ii) publisher text ingested from XML/HTML sources should normalize typographic spaces (`U+00A0`, `U+2005`, `U+2009`) explicitly during pre-ingestion curation, with `JSONPatch` events recording any normalization applied to quoted provenance.

=== 7.3 Limits of the evaluation and what remains to be measured
<limits-of-the-evaluation-and-what-remains-to-be-measured>
The evaluation in §6 supports claims about the commit gate. It does not yet support claims about discovery, and five limits should be kept in view.

+ #strong[Exploration is not evaluated.] No experiment places a CEG in a model’s context and scores the hypotheses that the model produces. The dossiers of the production corpus (§6.9) were written through rounds of language-model review, but this paper does not score them: no recall, precision, or discovery rate is measured. Whether in-context exploration over a CEG finds real moderating variables, and how often its hypotheses survive testing, is the central open question.
+ #strong[Size is measured, but fit in a context window is not.] Two corpora have been measured. In canonical JSON, the 31 `EvidenceAtom`s of §6.8 occupy 35,218 bytes in total, with a median of 1,155 bytes per atom (range 912 to 1,232). That is about 2.9 kB per study, with one to four curated outcomes per study. That density is not representative of production records: the settled production corpus of §6.9 averages 17.7 kB per study in the same canonical form, because about 12.3 outcomes were extracted per study, and its three single-window views occupy 3.2 to 9.6 million bytes each. The canonical form writes out every empty field and is not optimized for size. These figures describe record size only: the number of tokens depends on the model’s tokenizer, no model was run with a large graph in its context, and how reasoning quality changes as the graph grows is not measured. At a rule of thumb of 3 to 4 bytes per token, the smallest view is of the order of 0.8 to 1.1 million tokens and the largest of 2.4 to 3.2 million tokens.
+ #strong[The synthetic suites are in-sample.] The reference implementation was revised with access to every synthetic case (§6.1), so the synthetic results are regression checks and are not estimates of performance on unseen errors.
+ #strong[The real-text evaluation is small and curated.] It covers 12 studies, 31 findings, 36 verbatim sentences, and 84 synthesis records. The package contains no extraction pipeline: these records were curated for this evaluation, and the production corpus was extracted by a separate pipeline. Extraction at scale will introduce errors, and the checks cannot see an extraction error that is consistent with the rest of the extracted data (§7.1, item 1).
+ #strong[The production corpus is a prioritized subset that had not been certified at the time of the snapshot.] In this snapshot it holds 1,151 of the 3,246 records that advanced at screening, and 2,095 lower-priority records were not extracted. Its records were extracted and verified by language models; 3,014 outcomes (21.4%) are pinned to an abstract rather than to full-text results, 4,949 outcomes (35.1%) need figure digitization, 8,580 outcomes (60.8%) are typed as qualitative, and none of the 14,105 outcomes is certified against its primary source. Its dossier records were checked by the project’s own validator and not by the reference implementation, and only 139 of its 1,565 cited outcome tuples carry a point-level quantity. The same holds for the ten findings of §5: their quoted sentences were located in publisher-deposited text, but none is certified against a primary PDF, so no exact quantitative promotion is available in that bundle either.

== 8. Conclusion
<conclusion>
Context–Evidence Graphs give researchers and language models a way to load a large structured literature into context, explore it freely, and generate hypotheses and experimental bridges that stay tied to the recorded findings. Verification serves that discovery work rather than restricting it: exploration is unconstrained, and a deterministic gate checks only what is written back, so the shared evidence graph stays clean as it grows. The gate is needed because common errors in automated quantitative synthesis recombine real citations and real numbers across incompatible outcomes, arms, units, bounds, and experimental contexts. CEGs separate what can be guaranteed deterministically (exact seven-coordinate typed propositions, invertible state-guarded curation ledgers, partial-order transfer gating across incomparable models, and non-prose metadata surface locks) from heuristic prose linting, whose impossibility bounds are proved and whose verbatim clinical refusal rates are measured explicitly. This paper formalizes the representation and benchmarks that integrity layer, and a production corpus of 1,151 type 1 diabetes studies shows the representation and its correction log operating at that scale. Measuring how much of such a corpus a model can use in its context window, and how well exploration over a CEG yields sound hypotheses, is the next step.

== Code and Data Availability
<code-and-data-availability>
The zero-dependency reference implementation (`ceg_reference.py` `v3.1`), domain vignettes (`domain_vignettes.py`), synthetic benchmark (`ceg_synthetic_benchmark.py` and `benchmark_results.json`), 12-study PubMed real-text evaluation (`ceg_real_text_evaluation.py` and `real_text_results.json`), and primary bibliography ledger (`audit/BIBLIOGRAPHY-PRIMARY-LEDGER.json`) are included in the standalone companion package (#link("https://github.com/openislet/ceg")\; overview and guide at #link("https://openislet.org/ceg")) and require only the Python standard library. Deterministic byte-identical JSON regeneration has been verified across CPython `3.9`–`3.14` under both normal and `-O` execution. The type 1 diabetes worked example of §5 is built by `domain_vignettes.build_t1d_vignette`, which also holds the synthetic `NX-410` bundle used by the benchmark; `t1d/check_t1d_vignette.py` rebuilds it and compares every finding with its record in the OpenIslet corpus, while its `--quotes` option locates each quoted sentence in the article text held by the National Center for Biotechnology Information (stored outputs `t1d/t1d_vignette_verification.json` and `t1d/t1d_vignette_quote_check.json`). The production-corpus figures of §6.9 are produced from the OpenIslet snapshot files by `t1d/t1d_corpus_metrics.py` and `t1d/replay_production_ledger.py`, whose `--recompute` option recomputes them except for the counts reported by that project’s own validator and its certification status, which are read from the snapshot’s own records; their stored outputs (`t1d/t1d_corpus_metrics.json` and `t1d/production_ledger_replay.json`) are included. The settled 1,151-study type 1 diabetes Context–Evidence Graph is publicly available under CC BY 4.0 in the OpenIslet repository (#link("https://github.com/openislet/openislet")\; #link("https://openislet.org/blueprint")). The pre-export staging masters and curation ledger read by `--recompute` are not bundled in this package: the first and fourth master files are pinned by SHA-256 digests beginning 6f14f1ec627e and cb24687ac7a6, and the curation ledger by one beginning f28a2a22af7f (read-only staging archive available upon request from #link("mailto:kev@openislet.org")[kev\@openislet.org], with the stated SHA-256 digests verifying byte identity on retrieval).

